Was the office your first lead role?
《办公室》是你的第一个主演角色吗？

315
00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:32.720
Yeah, I think it was.
对，我想是的。

316
00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:34.220
I had done.
我演过。

317
00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:37.480
It was a, it was really fun to do.
那是部，拍起来真的很有趣。

318
00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:44.600
I, but I played a crazy Greek chef on a sitcom with Annie Potts and Tim Curry.
我，但我曾在一部由安妮·波茨和蒂姆·克里主演的情景喜剧里演过一个疯狂的希腊厨师。

319
00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:49.280
So that's, that's the, that's the sitcom that I had done.
所以那就是，那就是我演过的情景喜剧。

320
00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:57.320
And I did a, I did a show just before the office starring Tom Papa, a really funny comedian.
而且我在《办公室》之前还演过一个节目，由汤姆·帕帕主演，他是个非常搞笑的喜剧演员。

321
00:14:57.320 --> 00:15:02.200
And he had a show come to Papa and he was, he was so great.
他当时有个节目叫《来找帕帕》，他真的，真的很棒。

322
00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:05.440
And, and the show, you know, only lasted a few episodes.
而且这个节目，你知道的，只播了区区几集。

323
00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:12.660
So I, I went from that and then auditioned for the office when that, when that ended, but no, I'd never done a lead.
所以我，我从那个节目出来，等它结束时就去试镜了《办公室》，但不是，我从没演过主角。

324
00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:13.980
I'd never done a lead role.
我从没演过主角。

325
00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:20.620
Cause I, I have such a memory of you, Steve, sitting over on that little sofa at front reception or the chair.
因为我，史蒂夫，我对你有着深刻的记忆，你总是坐在前台接待处的那张小沙发或椅子上。

326
00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:27.280
Sometimes that was in front of Michael's office, just studying your lines because you, you would have to carry every scene.
有时是在迈克尔的办公室门口，一直在背台词，因为你得撑起每一个场景。

327
00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:32.880
And I just breezed through and said something snarky to Kevin and then breezed out, you know, for the most part.
而我基本上就是轻描淡写地走过去，对凯文说句刻薄的话，然后又轻飘飘地走开，你知道的。

328
00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:38.400
But I, I always thought, was there a lot of pressure to be the lead and have to carry all of that?
但我一直在想，作为主角必须扛下所有压力，会不会很大？

329
00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:44.440
Not, not, not with that group of people, because I felt like everyone was an equal component of that show.
不，和那群人在一起完全不会，因为我觉得每个人都是那部剧里同等重要的组成部分。

330
00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:45.400
I didn't feel that way.
我不这么觉得。

331
00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:51.300
I didn't feel like I was carrying any more load than anyone else.
我不觉得我比其他人承担了更多的负担。

332
00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:58.640
Because when you think about it, you know, yeah, I had a lot of dialogue, but everyone was there all the time.
因为仔细想想，对吧，我的台词确实很多，但每个人都一直在现场。

333
00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:04.260
And even if you were in the background of a scene, you're still acting, you're still active.
而且就算你在一个场景里当背景板，你其实也还在演，你依然在参与。

334
00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:10.800
You, like, all of us had character stuff going on at all times.
我们所有人，随时都有各自角色的事情在发生。

335
00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:22.600
So I, I don't think enough can be said for the fact that to be, to just be present on that set, everyone was working really hard.
所以我觉得，怎么夸都不过分的是，为了能在这个片场呈现状态，每个人都在付出巨大的努力。

336
00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:26.740
But it didn't, it didn't, I don't, I don't want to make it sound like it was hard work because it wasn't.
但这并没有，我不想，我不想把它说得好像工作有多苦，因为其实并不苦。

337
00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:27.860
It was really fun.
真的很有趣。

338
00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:28.320
Right.
没错。

339
00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:30.400
Because everyone was there supporting each other.
因为每个人都在互相支持。

340
00:16:30.400 --> 00:16:34.660
So no, I never felt, I never felt like any undue pressure.
所以不，我从来没有，我从来没有觉得有什么不合常理的压力。

341
00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:50.420
And also the writing was so good that at a certain point it became very easy to memorize lines because you started to understand exactly how the writers were writing it and how they were also writing to us,
而且剧本写得太好了，到了一定阶段背台词变得非常轻松，因为你开始完全理解编剧是怎么写的，他们又是如何根据我们来创作的，

342
00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:53.300
like to our characters and to all of our voices.
就像是针对我们的角色和我们各自的声线那样。

343
00:16:54.320 --> 00:17:00.720
So I find that if dialogue's really good, it's generally pretty easy to remember because it makes sense.
所以我发现如果台词真的很好，通常是很容易记住的，因为它合乎逻辑。

344
00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:01.400
That's right.
说得对。

345
00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:02.960
I always think about that too.
我一直也是这么想的。

346
00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:04.680
If it's well-written, I have no problem.
如果剧本写得好，我就完全没问题。

347
00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:07.720
And then our joke is we'd never make it on a medical show.
然后我们开玩笑说，我们永远进不了医疗剧组。

348
00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:13.500
Because all of the things they have to just quickly rapid fire off.
因为他们那些台词都得像机关枪一样噼里啪啦快速连珠炮似地蹦出来。

349
00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:16.600
I'd be like, oh no, I'm not, I'm not going to audition for that.
我会觉得，哦不，我不去，我才不去试镜那种剧。

350
00:17:16.820 --> 00:17:18.020
It'll be a disaster.
那绝对是一场灾难。

351
00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:18.440
Yeah.
是啊。

352
00:17:18.700 --> 00:17:19.520
No, that would be tough.
不，那确实很难。

353
00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:34.640
So Steve, we have talked about many times on this podcast, just about our gratitude for your just stability and consistency and your leadership.
史蒂夫，我们在这个播客里多次谈到，非常感激你一直以来的稳定、一致和你的领导力。

354
00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:42.300
So much so that a fan wrote in Sarah H and said, we've heard how supportive and professional Steve is on set.
以至于有位粉丝莎拉·H（Sarah H）来信说，我们听说史蒂夫在片场是多么地支持他人、多么地专业。

355
00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:48.660
And I would love to hear Steve's perspective or advice on setting the tone at work and fostering a supportive environment.
我很想听听史蒂夫关于在工作中奠定基调和营造支持性环境的看法或建议。

356
00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:51.380
Let's start by saying, I am pretty great.
首先我要说，我其实挺棒的。

357
00:17:53.460 --> 00:17:55.380
So some of it's just in your DNA.
所以有些东西是刻在你的DNA里的。

358
00:17:55.380 --> 00:17:58.760
I mean, you had a shirt you would wear that said, I'm fantastic, right?
我是说，你以前穿过一件上面写着“我很棒”的衬衫，对吧？

359
00:17:59.240 --> 00:17:59.960
That's right.
没错。

360
00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:01.740
A shirt with my face on it.
一件印有我脸的衬衫。

361
00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:04.780
So just to reiterate that it's me.
所以只是为了重申那就是我。

362
00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:06.980
That's really nice of you to say.
你能这么说真的太好了。

363
00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:13.360
But again, I felt like I was part of this very unique ensemble.
但话说回来，我觉得自己是这个非常独特的演员阵容的一部分。

364
00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:16.820
And so I never thought of myself that way.
所以我从来没有那样想过自己。

365
00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:23.660
I thought of Greg as our leader and our sort of mentor in terms of this world.
在这个世界里，我认为格雷格是我们的领导者，也是我们某种程度上的导师。

366
00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:26.320
And he oversaw everything.
他统筹着一切。

367
00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:29.320
And everyone was so nice.
而且每个人都很好。

368
00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:36.700
Everyone was so kind to each other that I never got a sense that it should be any other way than the,
每个人对彼此都那么亲切，以至于我从未觉得事情本该是别的样子，

369
00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:48.440
just in terms of the production of the show, like from wardrobe to the writers, to all of the crew, you know, catering, craft service.
就节目的制作而言，从服装到编剧，再到所有的工作人员，你知道的，餐饮、后勤服务。

370
00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:50.860
Everybody felt like they were a part of something.
每个人都觉得自己是团队的一部分。

371
00:18:50.860 --> 00:19:03.520
And the other part of it is, I believe sincerely that every one of us knew, no matter who watched it or whether it had any sort of longevity in terms of viewership,
另一部分原因是，我深信我们每个人都知道，无论谁来看，或者它在收视率方面能维持多久，

372
00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:08.580
even if, you know, if we got canceled after a season or two or three, I was so,
即使，你知道的，如果我们演了一两季或三季就被砍了，我也会，

373
00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:17.320
I think we were all so happy and thankful for the time that we got to work on the show and to be with one another.
我想我们大家都很高兴，也很感谢能有时间参与这部剧，并和彼此在一起。

374
00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:20.820
It was really, it just, it was a special, a special thing.
真的，这感觉，这是一件很特别的事。

375
00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:24.020
And we were aware of how special it was in the moment, I think.
我想我们当时就已经意识到它是多么特别了。

376
00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:25.160
For sure.
确实如此。

377
00:19:25.380 --> 00:19:25.920
For sure.
确实如此。

378
00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:38.100
I always was just super thankful for the writing because it was like, you know, when you do an improv show or a sketch comedy show or something,
我一直对剧本超级感激，因为你知道，当你做即兴喜剧秀或素描喜剧秀之类的时候，

379
00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:42.660
and there's one person that wants the joke, wants the joke, wants the joke, and they just kill the show.
总会有人非要抢包袱、抢包袱、抢包袱，结果把节目给毁了。

380
00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:44.920
I never felt that on our show.
我在我们的剧里从没有过这种感觉。

381
00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:51.040
It was like, if Phyllis had the best button, she could level the room or Oscar or Creed.
就像如果菲莉丝有一个最好的收尾包袱，她能把全场逗翻，或者奥斯卡，或者克里德。

382
00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:51.800
It didn't matter.
这都无所谓。

383
00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:54.100
Like the jokes came from everyone.
笑料来自每一个人。

384
00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:55.940
It was a real creative collective.
这是一个真正的创意集体。

385
00:19:56.020 --> 00:19:57.560
And the writers wrote that way.
编剧也是这样写的。

386
00:19:57.720 --> 00:19:59.160
And Greg treated us that way.
格雷格也是这样对待我们的。

387
00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:02.040
Like Greg could look at a scene and be like, no, you know what?
比如格雷格看一场戏时会说，不，你知道吗？

388
00:20:02.120 --> 00:20:04.460
It's not working with Jim getting the laugh.
让吉姆来抖包袱效果不太好。

389
00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:07.820
Let's flip that and let's have Stanley say it.
我们换个方式，让斯坦利说这句词。

390
00:20:07.940 --> 00:20:08.080
Right.
对。

391
00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:09.680
And then it just floored everyone.
然后这让所有人惊艳不已。

392
00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:11.620
And so I always felt that way.
所以我一直都有这种感受。

393
00:20:11.660 --> 00:20:15.280
And I feel like Greg set that tone for us that like everybody gets to play.
我觉得格雷格为我们定下了这个基调，那就是每个人都有上场机会。

394
00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:16.840
You're exactly right.
你说的完全正确。

395
00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:19.860
Everyone got their shot.
每个人都有自己的高光时刻。

396
00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:23.500
Not just that, but everyone else loved it.
不仅如此，其他人也都乐见其成。

397
00:20:23.620 --> 00:20:25.220
Everyone else was supportive of it.
其他人都对此表示支持。

398
00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:28.580
There was no resentment, no ego involved.
没有什么怨恨，也没有什么勾心斗角。

399
00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:31.260
And like, why not?
而且，为什么不呢？

400
00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:32.380
That's the way to do it.
这才是正确的方法。

401
00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:37.620
And I think you can see it when you watch the show because there is a calm to it.
我想你在看这部剧的时候也能看出来，因为里面有一种平静的氛围。

402
00:20:37.780 --> 00:20:41.180
Like no one's, everyone, it's all good.
就好像没有人，每个人，一切都很好。

403
00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:41.960
Yeah.
是的。

404
00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:47.640
Everyone's, everyone is, everyone's loving each other, you know, in terms of us as actors
每个人，每个人都，每个人都在互相关爱，你知道的，就我们作为演员而言

405
00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:50.360
and people beyond the characters.
以及角色之外的人。

406
00:20:50.660 --> 00:20:54.760
I get so excited for conference room scenes because those were our rapid fire things.
我对会议室的戏份感到非常兴奋，因为那些都是我们语速极快的对白。

407
00:20:55.100 --> 00:21:00.240
I mean, Michael is up there, but literally every single person in the room has either
我的意思是，迈克尔在上面，但房间里的每一个人，真的，要么

408
00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:04.020
a bit or a look, and it's just so fun to watch.
有一个包袱，要么有一个眼神，看的时候觉得太有意思了。

409
00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:05.580
Almost impossible to get through.
几乎没法顺利演完。

410
00:21:05.800 --> 00:21:07.300
Oh, we, and we didn't.
哦，我们，我们根本没法顺利演完。

411
00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:08.640
Oh my God, we laughed so much.
天哪，我们笑得太厉害了。

412
00:21:08.860 --> 00:21:13.860
Well, people are very excited for you to talk about some of your favorite memories of shooting
嗯，大家非常期待你谈谈拍摄《办公室》时的一些最难忘的回忆。

413
00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:14.300
The Office.
具体来说，他们很好奇你有没有最喜欢的一集，我觉得这就像是问，

414
00:21:15.100 --> 00:21:19.040
Specifically, they're curious if you have a favorite episode, which I think is like asking,
你有没有最喜欢的孩子？

415
00:21:19.180 --> 00:21:20.020
do you have a favorite child?
这太难了。

416
00:21:20.140 --> 00:21:20.840
It's very hard.
太难了。

417
00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:21.320
It's so hard.
是的，那确实很难。

418
00:21:21.620 --> 00:21:22.920
Yeah, that is hard.
你知道的，我的最后一集，讲到走心。

419
00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.900
You know, my last one, talk about heart.
那一集拍起来真的很不容易，但同时我也很喜欢，因为

420
00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:33.700
It was a really difficult episode to do, but I also loved it at the same time because
那可以说是迈克尔的终局。

421
00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:37.480
it was sort of that end game for Michael.
那是高潮，某种程度上展现了他的成长，他不需要那种隆重的欢送。

422
00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:45.000
It was that the culmination, the sort of showing the growth that he didn't need the big send
他不需要盛大的派对。

423
00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:45.160
off.
他可以用自己的方式向所有的朋友道别，而不需要任何，你知道的，

424
00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:46.500
He didn't need the big party.
大张旗鼓的庆祝。

425
00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:54.240
He could say goodbye to all of his friends on his own terms without any of the, you know,
没错。

426
00:21:54.380 --> 00:21:55.160
the fanfare.
所以，所以那个，我真的挺喜欢，你知道的，让那个角色在那个时刻进化到那种地步。

427
00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:55.540
Exactly.
人们总是会提到这一集，但是，嗯，不过《晚宴》这一集，也是数一数二的

428
00:21:56.200 --> 00:22:03.620
And, and so that, that I, I really enjoyed, you know, having that character evolve to that
而且，你知道的，我们在编剧罢工后回来了。

429
00:22:03.620 --> 00:22:04.620
point in that moment.
所以那是我们拍的第一场戏。

430
00:22:04.780 --> 00:22:11.520
And people always point to this one, but, um, but dinner party, dinner party's up there
我知道你们已经讨论过了，但我觉得大家都很渴望回到工作岗位，而且真的很

431
00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:15.480
and, you know, we'd come back after the writer's strike.
想念彼此。

432
00:22:15.620 --> 00:22:17.340
So that was the first thing that we had shot.
而且这一集其实已经在那里酝酿了一段时间，蓄势待发了。

433
00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:22.780
I know you've discussed it, but I think everyone was so ready to get back to work and had really
它非常诡异，主题上很厚重，很疯狂，还有很棒的客串演出和出色的视觉笑料。

434
00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:23.560
missed each other.
它在很多方面也很黑暗，非常悲伤，而且在情感上有点紧绷。

435
00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:29.840
And this is an episode that had just been sitting there waiting to happen for a while.
所以，那里面发生的事情真的很多。

436
00:22:30.500 --> 00:22:40.740
And it's so weird and, and kind of dense in thematically and crazy and had great guest
所以我也很喜欢那一集。

437
00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:43.860
appearances and great site gags.
我们直到复盘并搜索了一下才发现。

438
00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:50.860
It was also really dark in a lot of ways and very sad and kind of emotionally fraught.
显然它刚播出的时候票房惨败。

439
00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:53.900
So I, that, there was a lot going on in that one.
它并不是粉丝的最爱。

440
00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:55.260
So I, I liked that one too.
它不是今天这个样子，但是，嗯，大家其实并不买账。

441
00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:59.000
We didn't realize until we were breaking it down and we did a little Googling.
总的来说这部剧，你知道的，刚上映时也是遭遇惨败。

442
00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:01.980
Apparently it was a huge bomb when it first aired.
所以，你知道的，这大概需要慢慢去品味吧。

443
00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:04.060
It was not a fan favorite.
